<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">battlelog</ansi>

A battle plays out on its own, so when it goes badly the way to do better
next time is to see why. Every weapon blow your company throws or takes
keeps the engine's own roll, and <ansi fg="command">why</ansi> reads it back in plain words.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What a blow's breakdown says</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">1. Round 3: Aria -> the cutthroat captain, hit for 5.</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">   To hit: 60 in 100, rolled 12 (it landed). Skill and speed gave 55, +5 from company chemistry.</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">   Defence: 25 in 100 to turn it aside, and it was not.</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">   A telling blow.</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">   Damage: 7 before armor, armor 30 took 2, 5 got through.</ansi>

- <ansi fg="yellow">To hit</ansi> is the chance in 100 the blow was rolled at, and the roll, from 1 to
  100 (a roll at or under the chance lands). It names what moved the chance off what skill and
  speed gave: darkness or a second weapon, and company chemistry (<ansi fg="command">help evasion</ansi>,
  <ansi fg="command">help chemistry</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help light</ansi>).
- <ansi fg="yellow">Then it was blocked, parried or dodged</ansi> names the one defence the blow met
  and its chance in 100 (<ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi>). A defended blow does no damage.
- <ansi fg="yellow">A glancing, solid or telling blow</ansi> and <ansi fg="yellow">a critical hit</ansi> are said when they
  applied (<ansi fg="command">help narration</ansi>).
- <ansi fg="yellow">Damage</ansi> is the blow before armor, the armor it met and what that took, and
  what got through. Armor takes a random share of each blow, up to its rating
  in percent: armor 30 takes up to 29% of it. A blow armor took whole does no
  damage. A ward, aura or divine shield that took some says so on its own line.
- Named modifiers add a line each when they acted: a sharpened edge, a
  readied strike, Iaijutsu, a wind-up's multiplier, a piercing blow that
  ignored part of the armor, a ward or aura that took some, a coup de grace,
  class effects, leadroot poison, and hunger, thirst or a meal.
- A round with several blows (two weapons, a fast fighter) lists each as
  <ansi fg="yellow">Strike 1</ansi>, <ansi fg="yellow">Strike 2</ansi>. A blow that needed no roll says why: a Marksman's
  perfect shot, or a body with nothing to hurt with. A Swordmaster's Perfect
  Parry turns its blow without a roll, and says so. A pet's bite has a line
  of its own: what it did after armor. A chance pushed past the
  limits on any chance to hit says what it was held to.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Commands</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">why</ansi>            explain the last three rounds, newest first
  <ansi fg="command">why list</ansi>       number the recent rounds, one line each
  <ansi fg="command">why [number]</ansi>   explain one of them in full

It covers the latest fight (the log is cleared when the next begins, and
keeps the last 40 rounds), the rounds your own company fought, yours
and the foe's. Rounds against a foe you can't make out (in the dark, or
hidden) are left out: their armor and chances would give it away. The log
lives only while the server runs; a restart clears it. It works during a fight or after one, costs nothing, and
changes nothing. Spells and abilities are not explained here: their lines
say what they did.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">In the web client</ansi>

The Combat tab keeps a <ansi fg="yellow">Last rounds</ansi> list, under the battle and after it.
Each round is one line ("Round 3: the cutthroat captain → Wren: missed");
open it for the same breakdown. It keeps the last twelve of the latest fight.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">After the fight</ansi>

The battle summary also says why the fight went as it did
(<ansi fg="command">help battle-summary</ansi>): who took the most damage, which members never
landed a blow and what became of their swings, the class abilities the
company used, and the sigil that held over the battle.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help narration</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help battle-summary</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>.
